{#
  ABOUT THIS SITE. Footer link only, never the main nav: it is a colophon, and
  a colophon that competes with Contact for attention has misunderstood itself.

  THREE PARTS, ONE PAGE, IN ORDER. An essay saying what the site is for, a
  colophon saying what it is made of, and a scoreboard saying what that costs.
  Each one earns the next: the claim, the method, the evidence.

  ============================================================
  EVERY NUMBER ON THIS PAGE IS DERIVED OR MEASURED. NONE IS TYPED.
  ============================================================

  Two sources and the page labels which is which on every row.

  DERIVED, live, at build time: the article counts, straight off the
  collections. These cannot go stale, because they are recomputed on every
  build from the same data that builds the articles themselves.

  MEASURED, dated, from src/_data/siteStats.json: everything that needs a
  browser or a stopwatch. Bytes, requests, timings. Written by
  `npm run measure:stats`, which builds the site, serves it, loads every page in
  headless Chromium and records what actually arrived. Re-run it and the numbers
  move; that is the point.

  THE EMBARRASSING ONES ARE ON HERE TOO, which is the only reason the rest are
  worth reading. Three third-party requests on the median page and not one page
  at zero, because Lora is served by Google Fonts. A worst-case CLS above zero
  on every page. A median page weight north of 300 KB, most of it the six
  self-hosted weights of the display face. A reader with devtools open will find
  all three in about a minute, and a scoreboard that had quietly omitted them
  would be worth nothing.

  NO NEW TOKENS. The layout has more personality than the marketing pages,
  which the brief allows, but every value in about-this-site.css resolves to a
  token that already exists.
#}

{% call container(class="cs-about") %}

  {#- ---- 1. THE ESSAY ---- -#}
  {#- ss01, THE CROSSBAR-LESS A.

      NO ADJACENCY RULE APPLIES. DESIGN_SYSTEM.md scopes the O device by
      nearness to the isotype and says nothing of the kind about ss01, which is
      correct: the wordmark carries the crossbar-less A directly beside the icon
      in the masthead on every page of the site. The heading is 36px, well clear
      of the 15px floor below which the alternate stops reading as a letterform
      and starts reading as a broken A.

      ONE SWAP, AND IT IS THE WEAKER OF THE TWO ON THIS PAGE. The bar the
      heading macro sets is that the letterform reinforces what the word means,
      and who-we-are enforced it by dropping a swap from its "The Team" h2 on
      the grounds that a crossbar-less A says nothing about a team. The
      justification here is that this is the page where the site explains its own
      construction, the crossbar-less A is the letterform that most identifies
      the face it is set in, and the first character of the document is where
      the typeface introduces itself. If that reads as thin, drop it: zero swaps
      is always fine and the O below stands on its own.

      IT GOES THROUGH THE MACRO, which is the enforcement path. glyphSwaps
      checks the cap of two, checks the tag is h1 or h2, and checks the letter
      falls inside the named word, then warns and renders plain rather than
      failing silently. -#}
  <header class="cs-about__head">
    <p class="cs-eyebrow">Colophon</p>
    {{ heading("About this site", as="h1", class="cs-about__title",
               swaps={ word: "About", letter: "A", flavor: "alt-soft" }) }}
  </header>

  <div class="cs-about__essay">
    <p>
      We used to run this site the way we run a client's. A service page for
      every service, titles built to be found, the words arranged so a search
      engine would know exactly which aisle we were standing in. That is real
      work and we still do it, because plenty of clients need to be found and
      being found is the job.
    </p>
    {#- 2011 AND THE LINK IS THE JOKE. No description of the earlier versions:
        a reader who wants to know can look, and telling them first would spend
        the punchline. -#}
    <p>
      It has been through a few versions since 2011.
      <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20260000000000*/circulationstudio.com">They
      are all still on the Wayback Machine</a>.
    </p>

    {#- THE 2011 CAPTURE.

        HAND-WRITTEN MARKUP, NOT THE image SHORTCODE. This is a page template
        and the brief for it said no shortcodes, which is also how every other
        page template here references imagery. It would not have worked anyway:
        the shortcode requires an orientation token in the filename and this
        file carries none. See the note in the report.

        WIDTH AND HEIGHT ARE THE FILE'S REAL PIXELS, 560 by 503, read off the
        asset on the CDN rather than guessed. They are what reserves the box
        before the image arrives, and this page publishes its own worst-case
        layout shift two sections down. An image that moved the copy under it
        would be visible in a number the page prints about itself.

        THEY WERE 1594 BY 1432 UNTIL THE ASSET WAS RE-EXPORTED. The old file was
        96,640 bytes to draw at 280px, roughly six times the pixels needed. It
        is now 20,464 bytes at 560 wide, which is 2x for retina at the size it
        actually renders. Fixed at the source rather than by turning on a CDN
        transform layer: an optimizer would have added a moving part to every
        asset in the zone, and this page's whole argument is that there are not
        any. A correctly sized export needs one.

        LAZY, AND THE ONE FACT THAT COMPLICATES IT IS RECORDED HERE. The image
        sits at y 618, which is inside the first viewport at every laptop height
        measured: 1440 by 900, by 800 and by 720. So lazy is not free the way it
        is for something below the fold. The browser still fetches it, just
        later, after layout, which moves the paint of something a reader is
        already looking at rather than removing it.

        It is the right call anyway at this size. The asset is 20 KB and draws
        at 280px, so the deferred fetch costs a fraction of a second on a
        thumbnail nobody is reading the page for, and it keeps the first paint
        for the copy. The fetchpriority and the front-matter preload came off
        earlier for the same reason: those are the treatment for an LCP element
        and this stopped being one when it stopped being full width.

        THE CAPTION DOES NOT DESCRIBE THE DESIGN. The Wayback link above it is
        the joke and describing the old site would spend it. The ALT TEXT does
        describe it, because that is a different job: it is the only route a
        reader who cannot see the image has to the same thing. -#}
    <figure class="cs-about__og">
      <img src="{{ site.cdn }}the-og-circulation-studio.webp"
           alt="The circulationstudio.com home page as the Wayback Machine captured it on 9 October 2011. Concentric grey and dark red rings fill the left half behind the words circulation studio, now open. A stacked lowercase navigation runs down the right: home, web design, seo, photo, print media, news, about us, portfolio, contact us. Below sit four columns headed Web Design, SEO, Photography and Customer Service."
           width="560" height="503" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
      <figcaption>The first one, captured October 2011.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      We have been lucky enough to stay busy through clients and friends who
      send people our way. Which means this site does not have to go out and get
      the work. It only has to be true.
    </p>
    <p>
      So it turned into the place we get to build properly. Tooling we wanted an
      excuse to try, design decisions argued down to the last pixel, and a way of
      writing the work up that sounds like people talking rather than a brochure.
      We closed the social accounts. We have never advertised. Nobody here has to
      write "Welcome to our Laguna Beach SEO Agency in Laguna Beach" ever again.
    </p>
    <p>
      One honest exception. The Yelp material is not marketing for the work. It
      is the work, written down, with the parts we are still unsure about marked
      as unsure.
    </p>
  </div>

